#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f14d5ba4-85b6-520a-8734-6e7f0a153e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292098} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca0e2ed-49f6-5181-9312-902ef85eb1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292099} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc7d760-1a3e-55dd-8314-3c9b3f135582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292100} {:answer/id #uuid "af9dc71d-e91f-5d2b-bf3e-298a5b0c44bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292101} {:answer/id #uuid "42a1e879-dc0a-5af0-be45-86a3eb7cbace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f14d5ba4-85b6-520a-8734-6e7f0a153e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292098} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c1f251a-b759-512b-aeed-c7833b5ff71d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b103e245-fa8b-566e-aedb-314d0caff13b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106464" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-5)^{2}=2(x-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,5).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-5=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p(x-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p & =2 \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y-5=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-5=t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee9711b-2f58-5f63-8f70-bbcc2182287d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=-8(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292105} {:answer/id #uuid "c6416754-c8d9-5f89-8385-0eb9c969c480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-5)^{2}=8(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292106} {:answer/id #uuid "1bcd83f9-d129-5bd3-b4b3-a0cb6de23969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-5)^{2}=-8(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292107} {:answer/id #uuid "80ceae45-28dc-5749-a076-5b46dd832dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292108} {:answer/id #uuid "a4169520-377a-597f-a07d-944f9951066d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ceae45-28dc-5749-a076-5b46dd832dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292108} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a00b481-bf85-58c8-b8ac-4381399f3886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2830887f-a29d-5706-9c2e-a6b72884e503" :question/math-academy-id "q-106502" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=-4t,\\,y-5=-2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-2}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-2}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{y-5}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-8(y-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0ce42e4-a7d5-57da-b3dd-eb27f70938d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0ce42e4-a7d5-57da-b3dd-eb27f70938d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357192} :answer-field/id #uuid "767456eb-e8d8-5700-beb7-2079e41ff6b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40743546-cb8e-5497-97a4-a66504271cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40743546-cb8e-5497-97a4-a66504271cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357194} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ea54b2-2395-5bdf-9780-650ffe63c4ca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f54dfea7-1ce0-53bf-b599-41fdc4b47216" :question/math-academy-id "q-323209" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx+7=-3t,\\,y-2=-9{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+7}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2) \\\\ {(x+7)}^{2} & =-(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be34287b-38e7-5170-a71a-0eb0e221920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 1 =-\\frac{5}{2}t^{2}, y - 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155945} {:answer/id #uuid "65ee5c9b-fa3f-5800-b75b-cebca5de11e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 1 = \\frac{5}{2}t^{2}, y - 1 = \\frac{5}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155946} {:answer/id #uuid "66ffe0b0-422d-54db-84e3-ed0f5b8c6f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 1 =-\\frac{5}{2}t, y - 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}t^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155947} {:answer/id #uuid "7643eece-5e67-5948-90e0-98bcc2d7e897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 1 = \\frac{5}{2}t^{2}, y + 1 = \\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155948} {:answer/id #uuid "7cace0de-e79d-5563-bf8d-f16207edd5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}t^{2}, y - 1 =-\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cace0de-e79d-5563-bf8d-f16207edd5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}t^{2}, y - 1 =-\\frac{5}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155949} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccb64bb7-fe1d-5b6e-a529-5372787a10bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9821351-2cd3-5e5a-a4b5-1b6135a99522" :question/math-academy-id "q-84462" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-1)^{2}=-5(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p(x-1) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p & =-5 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(-\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-\\frac{5}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ff3f0f9-6acc-5744-8c27-0f133f07fc64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)^{2} = \\frac{8}{3}(y - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155969} {:answer/id #uuid "a3754869-1761-5538-a640-85b9e53512c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} = 3(y + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155970} {:answer/id #uuid "f2274a4a-b510-5707-bb62-43b75f170556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)^{2} = 6(y - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155971} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6f05a0-22f4-5225-9a17-a8da0a9efa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} = \\frac{8}{3}(y + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155972} {:answer/id #uuid "6efa0cc9-7d53-584a-9495-50f589783246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} = 6(y + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6efa0cc9-7d53-584a-9495-50f589783246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} = 6(y + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155973} :answer-field/id #uuid "5684eea7-273c-57de-840b-2b1914ce10d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e4599b9-0663-5b78-a942-16f34dae42fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-84850" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=3t,\\,y+2=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-2}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y+2)}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
